{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"6f7842f552b4ba0eaa06c35cf0d876800f7a802235fedbbdd76a4e3ed60131a6","slug":"midlatitude-cyclone-occlusion","location":"/weather_technology/midlat_cyclone/midlatitude_cyclone_occlusion.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Occlusion","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"Kevin Goebbert","given":"Kevin","family":"Goebbert"},"name":"Kevin Goebbert","affiliations":["University of Tampa"],"id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"}],"license":{"content":{"id":"CC-BY-4.0","url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","name":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","note":"All non-code content is licensed under CC-BY-4.0","free":true,"CC":true},"code":{"id":"Apache-2.0","url":"https://opensource.org/licenses/Apache-2.0","name":"Apache License 2.0","note":"All code content is licensed under Apache-2.0","free":true,"osi":true}},"github":"https://github.com/kgoebber/metpy_thebook","edit_url":null,"numbering":{"title":{"enabled":false,"offset":2},"equation":{"enabled":false},"figure":{"enabled":true,"continue":true}},"keywords":["metpy","python","meteorology","weather"],"copyright":"2026","affiliations":[{"id":"University of Tampa","name":"University of Tampa"}],"settings":{"output_stderr":"remove-warn","output_matplotlib_strings":"remove"},"source_url":"https://github.com/kgoebber/metpy_thebook/blob/main/weather_technology/midlat_cyclone/midlatitude_cyclone_occlusion.md","thumbnail":"https://kgoebber.github.io/metpy_thebook/build/midlat_cyclone_occlu-30d1d6649a6626658876d1a82eae58ee.png","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"midlatitude_cyclone_occlusion.md","url":"https://kgoebber.github.io/metpy_thebook/build/midlatitude_cyclone_-3ebb789c16bbf4b36419fa547058c1e0.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Near the end of the life cycle of a mid-latitude cyclone the cold air\nthat has been on only the north side of the cyclone will now wrap\ncompletely around the center of the circulation (","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"wdrmvpbhXo"},{"type":"crossReference","kind":"figure","identifier":"mature","label":"mature","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fig. 27","key":"zgnkwTwZqW"}],"template":"Figure %s","enumerator":"27","resolved":true,"html_id":"mature","remote":true,"url":"/midlatitude-cyclone-mature","dataUrl":"/midlatitude-cyclone-mature.json","key":"LdgVOS1bKl"},{"type":"text","value":"). This, in\neffect, cuts off the cyclone from the moist, tropical airmass that has\nbeen bringing energy into the system. This process is called occlusion\n(e.g., cyclone wrap-up). A result of the occlusion processes the cold\nfront and warm front meet and the remaining wind shift (and possibly\nslight temperature gradient) can be defined an occluded front. At this\npoint the cyclone may dissipate or form a new low-pressure center at the\ntriple point of the cold front, warm front, and occluded front.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"Q7bfUHFKFb"}],"key":"Mq8qFEvMYg"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"https://kgoebber.github.io/metpy_thebook/build/midlat_cyclone_occlu-30d1d6649a6626658876d1a82eae58ee.png","alt":"Occlusion stage of a Mid-latitude Cyclone","width":"650px","align":"center","key":"lEFctd1T7c","urlSource":"../../images/midlat_cyclone_occlusion.png"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"captionNumber","kind":"figure","label":"occlusion","identifier":"occlusion","html_id":"occlusion","enumerator":"28","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure 28:","key":"HSkWicjGtl"}],"template":"Figure %s:","key":"fRvWI8tbqW"},{"type":"text","value":"Schematic representation of the occluded stage of a mid-latitude\ncyclone at the surface and 500 hPa with location of low-level\nfronts for spatial reference.","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"w7HFQx7QMD"}],"key":"xjSp6UWNZi"}],"key":"eLXeDCu6Ql"}],"label":"occlusion","identifier":"occlusion","enumerator":"28","html_id":"occlusion","key":"aXd0Tg4V7c"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Typically, around the time that there is no longer a westward tilt of\nthe cyclone with height the surface system will occlude (","position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":25,"column":1}},"key":"MecSmfkbeW"},{"type":"crossReference","kind":"figure","identifier":"mature","label":"mature","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fig. 27","key":"cTAEE5ynxp"}],"template":"Figure %s","enumerator":"27","resolved":true,"html_id":"mature","remote":true,"url":"/midlatitude-cyclone-mature","dataUrl":"/midlatitude-cyclone-mature.json","key":"oFp9BpScGz"},{"type":"text","value":"). The\ncyclone is said to be vertically stacked at this point. Depending on the\norientation of the cyclone, redevelopment may be possible at the triple\npoint of the cold, warm, and occluded fronts. The redevelopment occurs\nfrequently with storm systems, but eventually all systems will dissipate\nafter an occlusion.","position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":25,"column":1}},"key":"XglOr8cazq"}],"key":"uuWXG6FRos"}],"key":"SbMZNxm3Fg"}],"key":"B9Er7Y9s1p"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Mature Stage","url":"/midlatitude-cyclone-mature","group":"Introduction to Weather Technology using MetPy"},"next":{"title":"Precipitation","url":"/midlatitude-cyclone-precipitation","group":"Introduction to Weather Technology using MetPy"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}